    "case_id": "634",
    "case_summary": "A 46-year-old man with no medical history presented with:- 3-month history of worsening abdominal distension- Abdominal pain- Diarrhea- Weight loss (25 pounds)- Decreased stool caliber- Palpable left lower quadrant massNo reported melena or hematochezia. No family history of relevant conditions.\n\nPhysical examination revealed:- Palpable mass in the left lower quadrant of the abdomen\n\n1. Laboratory Tests:- Carcinoembryonic antigen: normal- Carbohydrate antigen 19-9: normal2. Imaging Studies:- Computed Tomography (Abdomen):  Title: CT Abdomen with contrast  Description: 10.1 cm × 12.2 cm × 11.0 cm heterogeneous proximal sigmoid mass without adjacent lymphadenopathy. No evidence of distant metastasis.3. Endoscopic Examination:  Title: Flexible Sigmoidoscopy  Description: Large fungating sigmoid mass with near-complete luminal obstruction4. Pathology Findings:- Surgical pathology showed high-grade, poorly differentiated neoplasm with invasion through muscularis propria to serosal surface- No lymphatic, venous, or perineural invasion- 6 of 14 lymph nodes positive for metastatic disease- Immunohistochemistry: positive for vimentin; negative for keratin, pankeratin, desmin, CD34, CDX2, SMA, CD117, CAM 5.2, S100, NUT, DOG1, BCOR, KRT OSCAR, SOX10, and CD31",
